ascii.doc:
ascii: character classification and conversion on ASCII codes.

     use ascii;
     print is_digit('7'), to_upper('q'), digit_value('9'), hex_value('F'), rot13('a');

Characters are integers (QScript 'x' literals). Codes outside 0..127 are
never letters, digits or punctuation.

bits.doc:
bits: bit manipulation on 64-bit words.

     use bits;
     print popcount(0xff), clz(1), rotl(1, 65), extract(0xabcd, 4, 8);

Bit 0 is the least significant. `>>` in QScript is a logical shift; use
sar() for an arithmetic (sign-propagating) shift. Shift counts must be in
0..63; rotl/rotr reduce theirs modulo 64.

checksum.doc:
checksum: error-detecting codes over byte streams and digit strings.

     use checksum;
     let c = CRC32_INIT;
     c = crc32_byte(c, '1'); c = crc32_byte(c, '2'); c = crc32_byte(c, '3');
     print crc32_final(c), luhn_valid(79927398713), luhn_digit(7992739871);

Streams are folded one byte at a time, threading the state.

codec.doc:
codec: integer encodings.

     use codec;
     print zigzag(-3), unzigzag(5), gray(6), ungray(5), bcd(1234) == 0x1234, varint_size(300);
     // 5 -3 5 6 1 2

color.doc:
color: 24-bit RGB colors packed as 0xRRGGBB.

     use color;
     let orange = rgb(255, 165, 0);
     print red(orange), luminance(orange), mix(orange, WHITE, 128) == rgb(255, 210, 128);

Channels are 0..255; mix() weights run 0 (all a) .. 255 (all b).

combin.doc:
combin: combinatorics and classic integer sequences.

     use combin;
     print factorial(10), binomial(52, 5), catalan(10), fibonacci(90);

Results wrap past int64: factorial is exact up to 20, fibonacci up to 92,
binomial while every intermediate C(n-k+i, i) * (n-k+i+1) fits.

digits.doc:
digits: decimal digits of integers.

     use digits;
     print count(12345), sum(999), reverse(1230), is_palindrome(12321), digit(9876, 2);

Functions work on the absolute value unless noted; reverse keeps the sign.

hash.doc:
hash: non-cryptographic hashing of integers and byte streams.

     use hash;
     let h = FNV_OFFSET;
     h = fnv1a_byte(h, 'h'); h = fnv1a_byte(h, 'i');
     print h, mix64(42), combine(hash_int(1), 2), bucket(h, 16);

*_byte functions fold one byte (the low 8 bits) into a running state, so
streams are hashed by threading the state through calls.

logic.doc:
logic: boolean helpers. Any non-zero integer is true; results are 0 or 1.

     use logic;
     print xor(2, 0), implies(0, 5), majority(1, 0, 1), cmp(3, 7), select(1, 10, 20);

(`and`, `or` and `not` are operators, so they are not functions here.)

ntheory.doc:
ntheory: modular arithmetic and number theory, overflow-safe.

     use ntheory;
     print mod_pow(2, 100, 1000000007), mod_inv(3, 11), is_probable_prime(2305843009213693951);

Moduli are positive. mod_add/mod_mul never overflow, so any modulus up to
INT_MAX works. is_probable_prime is a deterministic Miller-Rabin test,
exact for every int64.

pack.doc:
pack: packing several small integers into one 64-bit word.

     use pack;
     let p = pack32(-5, 7);
     print hi32(p), lo32(p), byte(0x1234, 1), sign_extend(0xff, 8);   // -5 7 18 -1

QScript has no arrays or structs; packing is how libraries return or
store several values at once (see date::civil, color::rgb).

prelude.doc:
prelude: shared integer constants.

     use prelude;
     return INT_MAX;

Constants cost nothing at run time: they are folded into the code that
uses them and occupy none of the 16 variable registers.

prime.doc:
prime: primality and factorization by trial division.

     use prime;
     print is_prime(97), next_prime(100), nth_prime(10), totient(36);

Trial division is exact for every int64 but slows down for large
primes; ntheory::is_probable_prime is the fast deterministic test.

random.doc:
random: deterministic pseudo-random numbers with explicit state.

     use random;
     let s = seed(2024);
     for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) { s = step(s); print range(s, 1, 6); }

QScript has no global variables, so generators are pure functions: step()
advances a state, and value()/below()/range() derive outputs from a state
without changing it. The default generator is SplitMix64; xorshift and
lcg are provided as raw step functions. Not for cryptography.

range.doc:
range: closed integer intervals [lo, hi].

     use range;
     print contains(5, 1, 10), overlap(1, 5, 4, 9), wrap(13, 0, 9), sum(1, 100);  // 1 2 3 5050

seq.doc:
seq: figurate numbers and integer sequences.

     use seq;
     print triangular(10), is_square(144), collatz_steps(27), sigma(28), is_perfect(28);

testing.doc:
testing: assertion helpers for QScript test programs.

     use testing, prime;
     equal(2 + 2, 4);
     between(next_prime(10), 11, 13);
     let passed = check(0, is_prime(9) == 0, 1);   // counts passes, prints failure code 1

equal/not_equal/less/between/is_true/is_false print the offending values
and then trap with "assertion failed" (the failing line is reported).
check() is non-fatal: it prints `code` on failure and returns passed + 1
on success, so a program can count results.

time.doc:
time: seconds, clocks and Unix timestamps.

     use time;
     let t = unix(2000, 1, 1, 12, 30, 0);
     print t, clock(time_of_day(t)), weekday(t);   // 946729800 123000 6

Timestamps are seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC; clocks pack a time
of day as HHMMSS.
